WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.340
Let h of X encode the first row of a
circulant Hadamard matrix of order n.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.210
Its coefficients are
positive or negative one.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.180
The group ring identity says
that h times h star equals n,

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.800
with X to the n equal to one. We will
turn this polynomial identity into an

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.890
ordinary integer square by
substituting X equal to one.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:25.800
This substitution respects the cyclic
relation, because one to the n is one.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:31.010
Every positive power of one is one.
Every negative power is also one,

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.540
so reversing the exponents makes
no difference at this input.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.100
Consequently, h of one is
the sum of the row entries,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.320
and h star of one is exactly the same sum.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:46.380
Each entry is an integer, so this
common row sum is an integer.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:53.560
Evaluate the product identity. We obtain
n equal to h of one times h star of one,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.520
which is h of one squared. Therefore
the order is a perfect square.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.780
This conclusion comes from an equality
of integers, not just a congruence.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.592
For our original order four example, the
first row is negative one, one, one, one.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.580
Its sum is two, and two squared is four.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.420
The polynomial calculation agrees
with the size of the actual matrix.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.040
For a hypothetical
order thirty six matrix,

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.200
the row sum would have to be
positive six or negative six.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.120
This is a necessary condition, not
a construction of such a matrix.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.100
We have proved that n is a square.
For orders greater than one,

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:35.420
the next step uses orthogonality to
show that this square must also be even.
